ngs have kept the federal funds rate target at 3.50–3.75 percent following the July FOMC meeting, where three members dissented in favor of a 25-basis-point hike. Market-implied odds via CME FedWatch currently assign roughly a 55 percent probability of a quarter-point increase at the September 15–16 meeting versus a 45 percent chance of a hold, reflecting trader focus on July CPI due August 12 and subsequent labor market releases. Elevated energy prices tied to Middle East developments and the new chair’s emphasis on price stability above the 2 percent goal continue to shape hawkish sentiment, while moderating core inflation trends support expectations for a cautious path. The September decision and October follow-up remain key resolution points for any rate-hike contracts.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-12T14:01:38.782Z"},"version":"v1"}]
